{%- comment -%}
  The author card at the foot of a blog post.

  A post picks its author with one front matter key:

      author: courtney-hood

  Leave it out and you get whoever authors.json names as the default, which is
  Robert. That is why fifty existing posts needed no edit at all.

  Everything comes from src/_data/authors.json so a name, a job title or a
  photo is changed in exactly one place, not across fifty posts and three
  templates.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign authorSlug = author | default: authors.default -%}
{%- assign bio = false -%}
{%- for person in authors.people -%}
  {%- if person.slug == authorSlug -%}{%- assign bio = person -%}{%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}
{%- comment -%}
  An unknown slug falls back to the default rather than rendering an empty card,
  so a typo in front matter degrades to "written by Robert" instead of a hole in
  the page.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- unless bio -%}
  {%- for person in authors.people -%}
    {%- if person.slug == authors.default -%}{%- assign bio = person -%}{%- endif -%}
  {%- endfor -%}
{%- endunless -%}

<aside class="cf-author" aria-labelledby="cf-author-heading">
  <h2 id="cf-author-heading" class="cf-author-eyebrow">Written by</h2>

  <div class="cf-author-row">
    <a href="{{ bio.url }}" class="cf-author-avatar" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
      <img
        src="{{ bio.photo }}"
        alt=""
        width="420" height="420"
        loading="lazy" decoding="async"
      />
    </a>

    <div class="cf-author-text">
      <p class="cf-author-name">
        <a href="{{ bio.url }}">{{ bio.name }}</a>
      </p>
      <p class="cf-author-title">{{ bio.title }}</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</aside>
